@OBSThemeMeta {
    name: 'QCi';
    id: 'com.qci.QCi';
    extends: 'com.obsproject.Yami';
    author: 'JGh0stSecOps';
    dark: 'true';
}

/*
 * QCi -- the rig's own design language, worn by OBS.
 *
 * WHY THIS FILE EXTENDS YAMI RATHER THAN STANDING ALONE.
 * QCiApp_Themes.cpp permits a base theme (.obt) to name another base theme as its parent
 * (FindThemes() only rejects a base whose ancestor is NOT a base). Extending Yami means this
 * file inherits ~2700 lines of widget rules that already cover every dialog OBS ships, so
 * nothing falls back to a foreign colour, and it stays a single ADDITIVE file with zero merge
 * cost against upstream. QCi appears in Settings > Appearance as its own base theme because
 * isBaseTheme is decided by the .obt extension, not by the absence of `extends`.
 *
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE RULE, AND THE OVERCORRECTION THIS FILE USED TO BE.
 *
 * The previous version of this file made the whole application CYAN. Its reasoning was that the
 * preview contains QC, the window is behind the preview, and the rule from
 * ~/obs-assets/qci/qci-viz.html says nothing behind him may wear his green. That reasoning
 * turned a LOCAL rule into a global ban, and embody/palette.py names the mistake by name:
 *
 *     "The deep well (#0b3d1f) sits at ~152 and is legal, which matters: a threshold that
 *      refused it would have quietly turned a local rule into 'no green anywhere', and that
 *      overcorrection is exactly what QCi.obt did when it swung a whole application to cyan."
 *
 * The rule is about ADJACENCY. Its own token says so, verbatim from the operator:
 *
 *     qc-backing -- "the rest of it can be green just don't put the same color green directly
 *                    behind a qci"
 *
 * So exactly ONE surface in this file is forbidden green -- the preview background, which is
 * the pixels a rendered QC is composited onto -- and it is bound to that token by name below,
 * with the measured distance in the comment. Everything else wears green, because green is what
 * QCi looks like.
 *
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * EVERY COLOUR HAS A HOME. THERE ARE EXACTLY THREE, AND NO HEX IS CHOSEN HERE.
 *
 *   token:<name>   ~/Repositories/qci/branding/qci-palette.json, generated by
 *                  embody/tools/gen_palette.py from embody/palette.py. The palette says what a
 *                  colour MEANS.
 *   rig:<name>     ~/qci-rig/events/control.html's :root. The rig's seven-inch control panel
 *                  says what JOB each colour does: "STRUCTURE: borders, brackets, lit LED
 *                  cells, solid fills, OK" is --c-grn's own comment, and it is why the
 *                  structure hue here is green and not cyan.
 *   mix(a, b, t)   a linear per-channel blend, t of the way from a to b, where a and b are
 *                  themselves sourced. Alpha over an opaque backdrop IS a mix, so the rig's
 *                  rgba() surfaces arrive here as mixes with their compositing stated rather
 *                  than as pre-computed hexes nobody can check. `white` and `black` are allowed
 *                  as endpoints: they are the ends of the value axis, not hues.
 *
 * Every line below carries its source after `=`. lib/studiotheme.test.mjs in the rig recomputes
 * all of them and fails on any hex that cannot be derived, on any derived value that lands
 * within the palette's retype distance of a token it is not, and on any text pair that misses
 * AA. Nothing here was picked by eye, because nobody working on it can see it.
 *
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE SIX JOBS, as control.html declares them:
 *
 *   GREEN  #22c55e  STRUCTURE. Borders, brackets, lit LED cells, solid fills, OK. The chrome.
 *   MINT   #8cf9b4  LABELS. Headings, control labels, body copy, meter "hot". The type.
 *   CYAN   #22d3ee  NUMBERS, bolts, informational LEDs, THE FOCUS RING. Never a surface.
 *   PINK   #f02fb8  MONEY. Four spends, all of them a rendered currency figure, enumerated
 *                   at rule 15. Nothing else in this application may contain this string.
 *   AMBER  #f59e0b  ON AIR. )
 *   LAMP   #facc15  WARN.   ) three state hues, and none of them is ever a chrome surface.
 *   RED    #ef4444  FAULT.  )
 *
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THE PINK CONFLICT IS SETTLED, AND THIS FILE IS ONE OF THE TWO THAT CHANGED.
 *
 * lib/palette.mjs RIG_VS_PALETTE recorded a disagreement and instructed: "Resolve in one of
 * those two files, not in a third." It is resolved in favour of the palette, and control.html
 * is the other file that changes (--m-tip moves from --c-acc to --c-bad).
 *
 * The reasoning, because a ruling without one gets re-litigated: pink's entire value is
 * SCARCITY. The audio meter's tip and the LIVE indicator are two of the most persistently
 * visible pixels on a live-streaming rig, so spending money-pink on them puts it on screen
 * continuously and it therefore signals nothing — precisely the failure the token was written
 * to prevent. The two orphaned roles need no new token:
 *
 *   METER TIP / PEAK -> --danger #ef4444. A clipping meter IS a fault, and the Qt property is
 *                       literally named foregroundErrorColor. Binding the error colour to the
 *                       error hue is the correction, not a compromise.
 *   LIVE / ON AIR    -> --live #f59e0b, palette token `amber`, whose own role text reads
 *                       "terrarium heat-lamp core". BOTH firmwares already bind C_WARN to
 *                       QCI_AMBER for ON AIR, so the app is adopting the family's colour
 *                       rather than inventing one.
 *
 * CONSEQUENCE: the third yellow dies. #eab308 (the rig's --c-hot, and Yami's --warning) had no
 * palette home and is gone from this application; WARN is the palette's `lamp` #facc15. Two
 * yellows, both tokens, instead of three of which one was homeless. Amber and lamp sit 27
 * apart, which is close — but they are never adjacent and never the same FORM: ON AIR is a
 * large solid amber fill carrying --ink_on_fill; WARN is a thin lamp-coloured OUTLINE.
 *
 * #ffe82c -- QC's EYES -- does not appear in this file at all, and the rig's test suite checks
 * that it does not. It is his, and an application that spends it has taken something.
 *
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHAT THE .obt FORMAT CANNOT EXPRESS (for the layout overhaul to carry):
 *   - CORNER BRACKETS. SOLVED, and this line used to say they were unreachable. QSS has no
 *     ::before/::after, but border-image DOES a 9-slice: at 24x24 sliced 12/12/12/12 the edge
 *     and centre regions are zero-width, so only the four corner tiles paint. themes/QCi/
 *     bracket.svg draws two of them and leaves two empty, which IS the rig's two-corner frame.
 *     Applied to exactly three widgets at rule 16 — more than three and it stops being a
 *     signature rather than becoming one.
 *   - UPPERCASE, LETTER-SPACED LABELS. Qt QSS supports neither text-transform nor
 *     letter-spacing, and the rig's headings are both. This needs a QFont on the widget:
 *     ApplyLabelFont() in docks/QCiRigPanes.cpp is that font. It is a real limit, and it is
 *     why this file can set a SIZE and a FACE but not a CASE.
 *   - STEPPED MOTION. No @keyframes, no transition; nothing here animates, so the rig's
 *     steps(2,end) / 170ms grid / sub-3Hz photosensitivity constraint is not enforced here --
 *     it is simply not reachable.
 *   - DISCRETE LED CELLS. VolumeMeter paints a continuous bar in C++; only its colours are
 *     themeable. The rig's cell strip needs a meter subclass.
 */

@OBSThemeVars {
    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
     * GREEN -- THE STRUCTURE HUE.
     * Five of these six steps are a palette token or a rig var used at its
     * exact value; only green4 is derived, and it exists because Yami wants
     * a step between the deep green and the abyss that the palette does not
     * name.
     * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    --green1: #8CF9B4;  /* =c-lbl              LABELS. qci-token:green-bright -- QC's rim */
    --green2: #22C55E;  /* =c-grn              STRUCTURE. qci-token:green -- QC's body    */
    --green3: #16A34A;  /* =green-deep         pressed state and scrollbar  qci-token:green-deep */
    --green4: #117035;  /* =mix(green-deep, green-abyss, .5)   the step Yami wants and the palette lacks    */
    --green5: #0B3D1F;  /* =green-abyss        the deep well behind the tank  qci-token:green-abyss */
    --green6: #0F2A1B;  /* =m-unlit            the unlit LED cell  qci-token:stage-line */

    /* BLUE is Yami's --primary chain. QCi has ONE structure hue, so the blue
     * ramp is the green ramp -- aliased, not copied, so there is one place to
     * change and no second set of values to drift. */
    --blue1: var(--green1);
    --blue2: var(--green2);
    --blue3: var(--green3);
    --blue4: var(--green4);
    --blue5: var(--green5);
    --blue6: var(--green6);

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
     * CYAN -- NUMBERS AND THE FOCUS RING, never a surface.
     * control.html: "--c-num  NUMBERS, bolts, informational LEDs, the focus
     * ring". Yami's QSS never spends the teal ramp; it is defined so that a
     * plugin reaching for it lands on QCi's cyan rather than upstream's.
     * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    --teal1: #7AE5F5;  /* =mix(c-num, white, .40)   */
    --teal2: #22D3EE;  /* =c-num          the focus ring itself  qci-token:cyan */
    --teal3: #167788;  /* =mix(c-num, c-gnd, .45)   */
    --teal4: #125966;  /* =mix(c-num, c-gnd, .60)   */
    --teal5: #0D3841;  /* =mix(c-num, c-gnd, .76)   */
    --teal6: #0A1F26;  /* =mix(c-num, c-gnd, .88)   */

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
     * MAGENTA -- ACCENT ONLY. In this file it is spent on exactly one
     * thing: the top cell of the audio meter, which is what control.html
     * spends it on ("--m-tip: var(--c-acc)").
     *
     * NOTE A CONFLICT NOBODY HAS RESOLVED: qci-palette.json says of this hex
     * "MONEY ONLY, everywhere in the rig. Never structure", while
     * control.html lists "a meter's leading edge" among its jobs and then
     * uses it there. A meter tip is not money. This file follows the rig,
     * because the meter it is reproducing is the rig's; the disagreement is
     * recorded in lib/palette.mjs RIG_VS_PALETTE so it gets settled in one
     * of those two files rather than by a third opinion here.
     * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    --pink1: #F682D4;  /* =mix(c-acc, white, .40)   */
    --pink2: #F02FB8;  /* =c-acc          MONEY. qci-token:pink */
    --pink3: #931F73;  /* =mix(c-acc, c-gnd, .40)   */
    --pink4: #701959;  /* =mix(c-acc, c-gnd, .55)   */
    --pink5: #48123B;  /* =mix(c-acc, c-gnd, .72)   */
    --pink6: #280D23;  /* =mix(c-acc, c-gnd, .86)   */

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
     * THE TWO WARM STATES. Deliberately NOT #ffe82c: that is QC's eye
     * colour, and this file is checked for it by name.
     *
     * THIS RAMP USED TO HOLD ONE WARM STATE AND A SPARE, AND THE PANELS
     * HELD THE OTHER ONE. The app's warn was --c-hot #EAB308 and both ESP32
     * panels' was QCI_AMBER #F59E0B -- 23.9 apart, on the state an operator
     * reads at a glance while live, in an ecosystem whose whole instruction
     * was "the app, the scenes, the hub and the command center all follow
     * the same theme and color palette". The palette now publishes both
     * warm roles by name, so neither surface picks:
     *
     *   WARN  `lamp`  #FACC15  degraded link, stale poll, pre-clip dB
     *   LIVE  `amber` #F59E0B  ON AIR, and the lit light bar
     *
     * They are 47.3 apart and are never adjacent at large area -- live is a
     * banner, warn is a chip -- so "on air" can never be misread as "going
     * wrong". yellow3..6 are the WARN chip's plates and derive from the warn
     * hue, not from live, so a chip and its own type stay one colour family.
     * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    --yellow1: #FACC15;  /* =lamp                      WARN. 13.28:1 on the window  qci-token:lamp */
    --yellow2: #F59E0B;  /* =amber                     LIVE. ON AIR and the light bar  qci-token:amber */
    --yellow3: #9E8111;  /* =mix(c-hot, c-gnd, .38)    */
    --yellow4: #7C6610;  /* =mix(c-hot, c-gnd, .52)    */
    --yellow5: #4B3E0E;  /* =mix(c-hot, c-gnd, .72)    */
    --yellow6: #29230C;  /* =mix(c-hot, c-gnd, .86)    the Unassigned chip's plate */

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
     * RED -- FAULT, and the live/recording state. control.html: "hard
     * failure only -- OBS down, VOD alarm, refused".
     * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    --red1: #F58F8F;  /* =mix(c-bad, white, .40)   */
    --red2: #EF4444;  /* =c-bad          FAULT. 5.34:1 on the window  qci-token:fault */
    --red3: #A53032;  /* =mix(c-bad, c-gnd, .32)   */
    --red4: #802729;  /* =mix(c-bad, c-gnd, .48)   */
    --red5: #4D191C;  /* =mix(c-bad, c-gnd, .70)   */
    --red6: #271013;  /* =mix(c-bad, c-gnd, .86)   the Degraded chip's plate */

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
     * "PURPLE" IS NOT PURPLE HERE. Yami spends this ramp on exactly one
     * thing -- the HIDDEN source state in the mixer -- and control.html's
     * first instruction is "invent no hues". A seventh hue to say "hidden"
     * would be a hue with one user. Hidden is therefore DESATURATED: the
     * hairline green, dimmed, which is what a de-emphasised thing looks
     * like everywhere else in this rig.
     * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    --purple1: #508C68;  /* =mix(c-lbl, c-gnd, .45)    the rig's --c-dim, composited */
    --purple2: #296B47;  /* =mix(c-rule, c-gnd, .15)   */
    --purple3: #235A3C;  /* =mix(c-rule, c-gnd, .30)   */
    --purple4: #1D4832;  /* =mix(c-rule, c-gnd, .45)   */
    --purple5: #163426;  /* =mix(c-rule, c-gnd, .62)   */
    --purple6: #0D1815;  /* =mix(c-rule, c-gnd, .86)   the Hidden chip's plate: purple1 reads 4.57:1 on it */

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
     * SURFACES AND LINES.
     *
     * grey8..grey5 are the rig's four real surfaces, used verbatim and in
     * their own order. They are NOT neutral: the rig's near-blacks carry a
     * green cast, and a neutral substitute is the single change that makes
     * the whole application read as "a dark theme" instead of "this rig".
     *
     * grey4..grey1 are the LINES, brightening as their job gets louder. The
     * two that have to be FINDABLE were solved against the 3:1 non-text
     * threshold rather than picked: c-rule measures 4.00:1 on the window and
     * 3.82:1 on the field plate, which is the number control.html states for
     * it and the reason it exists.
     * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    --grey8: #030A06;  /* =s-hole                    inside a window: darker than the ground */
    --grey7: #07070B;  /* =c-gnd                     THE GROUND                              */
    --grey6: #08100F;  /* =s-well                    field and list plate                    */
    --grey5: #091612;  /* =s-block                   raised block: cards, property rows      */
    --grey4: #0F2A1B;  /* =m-unlit         row hover, slider groove  qci-token:stage-line */
    --grey3: #12532C;  /* =mix(c-grn, c-gnd, .60)    the rig's --s-edge, composited (2.20:1) */
    --grey2: #2F7D51;  /* =c-rule          component border, hairline  qci-token:rule */
    --grey1: #22C55E;  /* =c-grn           hover border, full strength  qci-token:green */

    /* TEXT. The rig's body copy is --c-lbl; control.html assigns it
     * "headings, control labels, body copy" in as many words. The ramp
     * descends toward the ground, and white5 -- which is --text_muted, the
     * dimmest type in the app -- was solved to clear 4.5:1 on the LIGHTEST
     * surface it can land on (--grey5, 4.66:1) rather than only on the
     * darkest. It lands exactly on the rig's own --c-dim. */
    --white1: #8CF9B4;  /* =c-lbl           qci-token:green-bright */
    --white2: #79D79C;  /* =mix(c-lbl, c-gnd, .14)    */
    --white3: #69BA88;  /* =mix(c-lbl, c-gnd, .26)    */
    --white4: #5FA77B;  /* =mix(c-lbl, c-gnd, .34)    */
    --white5: #508C68;  /* =mix(c-lbl, c-gnd, .45)    the rig's --c-dim (5.07:1 on the window) */

    /* INK. black1 is the rig's ONE ink for type on a solid fill; black3..5
     * are the disabled meter beds, which Yami paints in true greys and which
     * would be the only neutral pixels left in the application. */
    --black1: #04150B;  /* =c-ink           qci-token:on-accent */
    --black2: #030A06;  /* =s-hole                    */
    --black3: #193C2B;  /* =mix(c-rule, c-gnd, .55)   */
    --black4: #1F4E35;  /* =mix(c-rule, c-gnd, .40)   */
    --black5: #256040;  /* =mix(c-rule, c-gnd, .25)   */

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
     * TWO NAMES THIS THEME ADDS, because both are RULES rather than values
     * and a rule needs somewhere to live that is not a comment.
     * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

    /* THE ADJACENCY RULE. The one surface in this application that may not
     * be green, because a rendered QC is composited directly onto it. This
     * is the palette's `qc-backing` token at its exact value; it sits 204.4
     * from QC's body green, 224.5 from the LED body and 326.5 from his rim,
     * against a required margin of 96. */
    --qc_backing: #0A0A0F;  /* =qc-backing  qci-token:qc-backing */

    /* THE FOCUS RING. control.html gives cyan exactly one structural job and
     * this is it: ":focus-visible -- outline solid var(--c-num)". Every
     * focus state below points here, so the app has ONE focus colour and it
     * is not the same hue as anything else on screen. */
    --focus_ring: #22D3EE;  /* =c-num  qci-token:cyan */

    /* Type on a solid fill. control.html: "--c-ink -- the ONLY ink for type
     * on ANY solid fill". 8.25:1 on the structure green. It is NOT bound to
     * --text_light, because Yami spends that on the preview title label,
     * which sits on the window and would go invisible. */
    --ink_on_fill: var(--black1);

    /* ================================================================== *
     * TYPOGRAPHY. THIS FILE HAD NONE, AND THAT IS HALF THE VERDICT.
     *
     * `grep font QCi.obt` used to return nothing. Every size and every face
     * in the application was inherited Yami, which means the theme changed
     * what the window was COLOURED and nothing about what it READ like -- and
     * an operator looking at stock proportions in a new palette says, with
     * complete accuracy, "still looks like OBS".
     *
     * TWO FACES, BOTH VERIFIED PRESENT ON THIS MACHINE:
     *   LABEL   'Open Sans', '.AppleSystemUIFont'  -- Open Sans is installed but is NOT bundled
     *           (frontend/data has no fonts dir), so the system fallback is load-bearing and
     *           must stay. This is also the face Yami already asks for, so it is not a change;
     *           it is a statement so the next person does not have to go and look.
     *   NUMERIC 'Menlo' -- /System/Library/Fonts/Menlo.ttc, OS-guaranteed. SF Mono is
     *           dot-prefixed and unreliable to reach from Qt, which is why it is not used.
     *
     * A FACE IS NOT A VARIABLE HERE, AND THAT IS A PARSER FACT, NOT A STYLE CHOICE.
     * ParseThemeVariables() takes exactly ONE token as a value; a String value is produced by
     * cf_literal_to_str on that single token. So `--face: 'Open Sans', '.AppleSystemUIFont';`
     * would silently resolve to `Open Sans` and DROP the fallback -- the failure would be
     * invisible on this Mac (which has Open Sans) and total on one that does not. The QSS body
     * is not lexed, only var()-substituted textually, so the family list is written literally
     * in the rules below where the comma survives.
     *
     * THE SIZES SCALE WITH THE OPERATOR'S FONT SCALE, and the spec's literal pt values do not.
     * Appearance > Font Scale is a real setting he has set (FontScale=10), and a theme of
     * hard-coded pt makes that control a no-op -- the same class of defect as a border bound to
     * its own background. These are calc()s over --obsFontScale, which SetTheme() injects, and
     * which also sets currentTheme->usesFontScale so the control stays enabled. At his 10 they
     * resolve to exactly the specified 9 / 10 / 11 / 13 / 15 / 16 / 20pt.
     * ================================================================== */
    --font_micro:   calc(0.9pt * var(--obsFontScale));  /*  9pt  chip legends, lamp captions, delegate metadata */
    --font_label:   calc(1.0pt * var(--obsFontScale));  /* 10pt  control labels, pane titles, segments          */
    --font_body:    calc(1.1pt * var(--obsFontScale));  /* 11pt  list rows, chat, prose, viewer names, URLs     */
    --font_readout: calc(1.3pt * var(--obsFontScale));  /* 13pt  Menlo. every number: dB, telemetry, timecode   */
    --font_go:      calc(1.5pt * var(--obsFontScale));  /* 15pt  GO LIVE / ON AIR                               */
    --font_hero:    calc(1.6pt * var(--obsFontScale));  /* 16pt  PRIVACY HOLD                                   */
    --font_clock:   calc(2.0pt * var(--obsFontScale));  /* 20pt  Menlo. uptime and countdown ONLY               */

    /* ================================================================== *
     * SPACING. BASE UNIT 4, AND THE LIST IS CLOSED.
     *
     * The only legal values anywhere in a QCi-authored rule are these eight.
     * They exist as variables rather than as a comment for one reason: a grep
     * for a bare px in a padding or margin below now PROVES that no widget
     * invented a number, which a prose scale cannot do.
     *
     * --sp_6 is the one exception and it is stated rather than smuggled: a
     * 34px list row at --font_body needs 6px of vertical padding, 4 crowds it
     * and 8 overflows it. One exception, named, is a scale; an unnamed
     * seventh value appearing quietly is how a scale stops being one.
     * ================================================================== */
    --sp_0: 0px;
    --sp_2: 2px;
    --sp_4: 4px;
    --sp_6: 6px;    /* THE ONE EXCEPTION. List-row vertical padding. See above. */
    --sp_8: 8px;
    --sp_12: 12px;
    --sp_16: 16px;
    --sp_24: 24px;
    --sp_32: 32px;

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
     * SEMANTIC BINDINGS
     * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    --bg_window: var(--grey7);
    --bg_base: var(--grey6);
    --bg_preview: var(--qc_backing);

    --primary: var(--green3);
    --primary_light: var(--green2);
    --primary_lighter: var(--green1);
    --primary_dark: var(--green4);
    --primary_darker: var(--green5);

    /* Yami points --warning at yellow3, which is a fill-weight step being
     * used as TEXT by .text-warning: 2.55:1 on the window at this ramp.
     * Both move to the ramp's canonical state step: 13.28:1 and 5.34:1.
     *
     * --live is this theme's, not Yami's. It exists because ON AIR used to
     * be painted in --pink2, and pink is money: qci-palette.json says
     * "MONEY ONLY, everywhere in the rig", and an app that spends it on a
     * state has taken the one colour that means somebody paid. */
    --warning: var(--yellow1);
    --live: var(--yellow2);
    --danger: var(--red2);

    --text: var(--white1);          /* 15.63:1 on --bg_window */
    --text_light: var(--white1);
    --text_muted: var(--white5);    /* 5.07:1 on --bg_window  */
    --text_disabled: var(--text_muted);
    --text_inactive: var(--white3);

    --highlight_color: var(--focus_ring);
    --separator_hover: var(--focus_ring);

    /* Lines. Yami hangs --border_color off grey4, which in this ramp is a
     * FILL and would make every frame invisible. */
    --border_color: var(--grey3);

    --scrollbar_bg: var(--grey7);
    --scrollbar_handle: var(--green3);   /* the palette's stated job for green-deep. 6.10:1 */
    --scrollbar_hover: var(--green2);
    --scrollbar_down: var(--green1);
    --scrollbar_border: var(--grey3);

    /* SQUARE. --rad is 0 in control.html: this rig does not round anything.
     * These are the only radius tokens Yami declares as literals; the other
     * two are calc()s off a height and are overridden with literals here. */
    --border_radius: 0px;
    --border_radius_small: 0px;
    --border_radius_large: 0px;
    --toggle_radius: 0px;
    --action_row_collapse_radius: 0px;

    /* Inputs. control.html's .fld is a plate with a hairline; on focus the
     * border takes the ring colour and the plate goes DARKER, which is what
     * .fld:focus does. */
    --input_bg: var(--grey6);
    --input_bg_hover: var(--grey6);
    --input_bg_focus: var(--grey8);
    --input_border: var(--grey2);          /* 3.82:1 on the plate */
    --input_border_hover: var(--grey1);
    --input_border_focus: var(--focus_ring);

    --list_item_bg_selected: var(--primary);
    --list_item_bg_hover: var(--grey4);

    /* Buttons. The rig's .btn is an OUTLINE that washes on hover and FILLS
     * on press: "background rgba(34,197,94,.17)" then "background var(--c-grn);
     * color var(--c-ink)". Yami's default hides the border by aliasing it to
     * the fill; that is undone here. */
    --button_bg: var(--grey6);
    --button_bg_hover: var(--grey4);       /* the .btn:hover wash, composited */
    --button_bg_down: var(--green2);       /* .btn:active -- a full green fill */
    --button_bg_disabled: var(--grey8);
    --button_border: var(--grey2);         /* 4.00:1 on the window */
    --button_border_hover: var(--grey1);
    --button_border_focus: var(--focus_ring);

    --tab_bg: var(--grey6);
    --tab_bg_hover: var(--grey4);
    --tab_bg_down: var(--primary);
    --tab_bg_disabled: var(--grey8);
    --tab_border: var(--grey3);
    --tab_border_hover: var(--grey1);
    --tab_border_focus: var(--focus_ring);
    --tab_border_selected: var(--grey1);

    /* Qt palette. Anything the stylesheet does not reach -- native menus,
     * tooltips, item-view fallbacks -- lands here, so it is bound too. */
    --palette_window: var(--bg_window);
    --palette_windowText: var(--text);
    --palette_base: var(--bg_base);

    --palette_light: var(--grey2);
    --palette_mid: var(--grey7);
    --palette_dark: var(--grey6);

    --palette_highlight: var(--primary);
    --palette_highlightedText: var(--ink_on_fill);

    --palette_text: var(--text);
    --palette_link: var(--focus_ring);
    --palette_linkVisited: var(--green1);

    --palette_button: var(--button_bg);
    --palette_buttonText: var(--text);

    --palette_text_active: var(--text);
    --palette_text_disabled: var(--text_disabled);
    --palette_text_inactive: var(--text_inactive);
}

/* ==================================================================== *
 * THE RULES THE VARIABLE SURFACE ALONE CANNOT REACH.
 * Each one is here because a Yami rule hard-codes a value, or because a
 * pair that Yami never had to think about becomes unreadable once both
 * halves of it are green.
 * ==================================================================== */

/* 1. INK ON EVERY GREEN FILL.
 *    Yami never sets a foreground on its selected/checked states, so they
 *    inherit --text. Once --primary and --text are the same hue that is
 *    mint on green: 2.56:1, which is not a colour scheme, it is a bug you
 *    can only find by measuring. control.html's answer is the one used
 *    here -- "--c-ink, the ONLY ink for type on ANY solid fill" -- and it
 *    measures 5.70:1 on --primary and 8.25:1 on --primary_light.
 *
 *    The :selected:hover selectors are listed explicitly because Yami sets
 *    `color` on them directly, and a shorter selector loses to a longer one
 *    in Qt no matter which file it is in. */
QListView::item:selected,
QListWidget::item:selected,
QMenu::item:selected,
QTreeView::item:selected,
QTableView::item:selected,
SceneTree::item:selected,
QMenu::item:selected:hover,
QListView::item:selected:hover,
QListWidget::item:selected:hover,
QListWidget::item:selected:focus,
.list-grid SceneTree::item:selected,
.list-grid SceneTree::item:selected:hover,
.list-grid SceneTree::item:checked,
QPushButton:checked,
QPushButton:pressed,
QPushButton:pressed:hover,
.btn-tool:checked,
QTabBar::tab:selected,
QTabBar::tab:pressed,
QComboBox QAbstractItemView::item:selected,
idian--Row QComboBox QAbstractItemView::item:selected,
SourceSelectButton:checked,
.seg__opt:checked {
    color: var(--ink_on_fill);
}

/* 2. THE DOCK TITLEBAR IS THE RIG'S PANE HEADER, as far as QSS reaches.
 *    control.html frames a pane with a top-left and a bottom-right bracket
 *    and never four sides, and hangs a --c-rule hairline off the title.
 *    QSS has no pseudo-elements to draw a bracket with, so the header takes
 *    the one edge it can draw -- a 2px structure-green left rule -- plus
 *    that hairline underneath. It sits on the GROUND rather than on the
 *    button plate Yami gives it, so a docked pane reads as a region of the
 *    window instead of as a very wide button. */
QDockWidget::title {
    text-align: left;
    background-color: var(--bg_window);
    color: var(--text);
    padding: var(--padding_large);
    border: none;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--green2);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--grey2);
    border-radius: 0px;
}

/* The dock body: the rig's plate, hairlined, square. */
OBSDock > QWidget {
    background: var(--bg_base);
    border: 1px solid var(--grey3);
    border-top: none;
    border-radius: 0px;
}

/* 3. THE AUDIO METER IS THE RIG'S LED METER, PORTED.
 *    control.html section 8 declares the cells outright: --m-unlit #0f2a1b,
 *    --m-lit = --c-grn, --m-hot = --c-lbl, --m-tip = --c-acc, and it warns
 *    that amber "never appears in a meter cell; it appears in the dB READOUT
 *    near clip". So Yami's three zone BACKGROUNDS collapse to the single
 *    unlit cell colour -- the bed does not change colour under a bar in a
 *    real meter -- and the three foregrounds become lit / hot / tip.
 *    Measured against the unlit cell: 6.74:1, 11.94:1, 4.24:1.
 *
 *    magnitudeColor is a hardcoded rgb(0,0,0) upstream; the rig's ink is
 *    near-black green, so it is re-pointed. `color` is the dB tick LABEL,
 *    which Yami paints in grey3 -- a fill in this ramp, and 1.4:1 on the
 *    meter bed. */
VolumeMeter {
    color: var(--text_muted);

    qproperty-backgroundNominalColor: var(--green6);
    qproperty-backgroundWarningColor: var(--green6);
    qproperty-backgroundErrorColor: var(--green6);
    qproperty-foregroundNominalColor: var(--green2);
    qproperty-foregroundWarningColor: var(--green1);
    /* THE TIP IS NOT PINK ANY MORE, AND THE PROPERTY NAME IS THE ARGUMENT.
     * control.html had "--m-tip: var(--c-acc)" and this file followed it; the palette says of
     * that hex "MONEY ONLY, everywhere in the rig". A meter tip is not money, and a meter is
     * one of the most persistently visible objects on a live rig -- so spending the money
     * colour there put it on screen continuously and it stopped meaning anything, which is the
     * exact failure the token exists to prevent. The Qt property is literally named
     * foregroundErrorColor, a clipping meter IS a fault, and the rig already publishes red for
     * fault. Binding the error colour to the error hue is the correction, not a compromise.
     * 4.36:1 on the unlit cell. */
    qproperty-foregroundErrorColor: var(--danger);

    qproperty-backgroundNominalColorDisabled: var(--black3);
    qproperty-backgroundWarningColorDisabled: var(--black3);
    qproperty-backgroundErrorColorDisabled: var(--black3);
    qproperty-foregroundNominalColorDisabled: var(--white5);
    qproperty-foregroundWarningColorDisabled: var(--white5);
    qproperty-foregroundErrorColorDisabled: var(--white5);

    qproperty-magnitudeColor: var(--black1);
    qproperty-majorTickColor: var(--white4);
    qproperty-minorTickColor: var(--grey2);
}

/* 4. SQUARE CHECKBOXES. Yami's indicator SVGs are rounded rectangles in a
 *    fixed #B0AFB3, which is both a foreign hue and the wrong shape for a
 *    rig whose --rad is 0. Replaced with square, structure-hue ones that
 *    fill on check and carry the ink tick -- .tg[aria-checked] in
 *    control.html. Six files under themes/QCi/; no upstream file touched. */
QCheckBox::indicator:unchecked,
QGroupBox::indicator:unchecked,
QTableView::indicator:unchecked {
    image: url(theme:QCi/checkbox_unchecked.svg);
}

QCheckBox::indicator:unchecked:hover,
QGroupBox::indicator:unchecked:hover,
QTableView::indicator:unchecked:hover {
    image: url(theme:QCi/checkbox_unchecked_focus.svg);
}

QCheckBox::indicator:checked,
QGroupBox::indicator:checked,
QTableView::indicator:checked {
    image: url(theme:QCi/checkbox_checked.svg);
}

QCheckBox::indicator:checked:hover,
QGroupBox::indicator:checked:hover,
QTableView::indicator:checked:hover {
    image: url(theme:QCi/checkbox_checked_focus.svg);
}

QCheckBox::indicator:checked:disabled,
QGroupBox::indicator:checked:disabled,
QTableView::indicator:checked:disabled {
    image: url(theme:QCi/checkbox_checked_disabled.svg);
}

QCheckBox::indicator:unchecked:disabled,
QGroupBox::indicator:unchecked:disabled,
QTableView::indicator:unchecked:disabled {
    image: url(theme:QCi/checkbox_unchecked_disabled.svg);
}

/* The settings pages use idian's checkbox, which reaches for the same two
 * focus assets by path. */
idian--CheckBox.keyFocus::indicator,
idian--Row.hover > idian--CheckBox.row-buddy::indicator {
    image: url(theme:QCi/checkbox_unchecked_focus.svg);
}

idian--CheckBox.keyFocus.checked::indicator,
idian--Row.hover > idian--CheckBox.row-buddy.checked::indicator {
    image: url(theme:QCi/checkbox_checked_focus.svg);
}

/* 5. SLIDERS. The handle is a white pill upstream. Radius is already 0 from
 *    the variable above; the fill moves to the label mint, which is what the
 *    rig's fader thumb is -- a hard-edged block, not a knob -- and the two
 *    interaction states stop going DIMMER than the resting state, which is
 *    what Yami's white1 -> white4 -> white3 sequence does once those steps
 *    are a descending ramp. */
QSlider::handle {
    background-color: var(--white1);
}

QSlider::handle:hover {
    background-color: var(--focus_ring);
}

QSlider::handle:pressed {
    background-color: var(--green2);
}

QSlider::handle:disabled {
    background-color: var(--white5);
}

/* 6. THE STATS AND PROPERTIES TABLES. Yami draws gridlines in grey1, which
 *    in this ramp is full structure green -- correct for a hover border,
 *    far too loud for a grid nobody is meant to look at. */
QTableView {
    gridline-color: var(--grey3);
}

QHeaderView::section {
    background-color: var(--grey5);
    color: var(--text_muted);
    border: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--grey2);
    border-radius: 0px;
}

/* 7. THE THREE FRAMES ALONG THE BOTTOM OF THE MAIN WINDOW.
 *    Scene/source context, transitions and the stream controls are the
 *    panes a person spends the stream looking at, and Yami leaves all three
 *    on the bare window with no edge at all. They get the rig's plate and
 *    its hairline so they read as panes. */
#contextContainer,
#transitionsFrame,
#controlsFrame {
    background: var(--grey5);
    border: 1px solid var(--grey3);
    border-radius: 0px;
}

/* 8. THE PREVIEW LABELS sit directly above the one surface in this file that
 *    is not allowed to be green, and they are the caption on it. Mint on the
 *    window, not on the picture: 15.63:1. */
.label-preview-title {
    color: var(--text);
}

/* 9. STATUS BAR. Yami leaves it on the window with no separation from the
 *    docks above it. */
OBSBasicStatusBar {
    background: var(--grey7);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--grey3);
}

/* 10. TEXT INPUTS HAVE AN EDGE, WHICH UPSTREAM'S DO NOT.
 *     Yami writes `border: 1px solid var(--input_bg)` on QLineEdit, QPlainTextEdit,
 *     QComboBox and both spinboxes -- the border is bound to the FILL, so every text
 *     input in the application has a border that is exactly its own background. It
 *     measures 1.00:1, which is another way of saying there is no border. That is a
 *     defensible choice on a theme whose fields are lighter than their surroundings;
 *     it is not one here, where the field plate is a shade off the ground and the
 *     rig's own .fld carries a hairline. --input_border measures 3.82:1 on the plate.
 *
 *     The VALUE fields also take the number colour, which is control.html's .fld
 *     verbatim: "color: var(--c-num)", with select and textarea overriding back to the
 *     label mint. Cyan on the field plate is 10.65:1, and it is the single clearest
 *     signal in a settings dialog that this is not stock OBS. */
QLineEdit,
QTextEdit,
QPlainTextEdit,
QSpinBox,
QDoubleSpinBox,
QComboBox,
QDateTimeEdit {
    border: 1px solid var(--input_border);
}

QLineEdit,
QSpinBox,
QDoubleSpinBox,
QDateTimeEdit {
    color: var(--focus_ring);
}

/* 11. THE KEYBOARD FOCUS RING ON A SETTINGS ROW.
 *     `idian--Row.keyFocus` upstream is `background: var(--grey4); border: 1px solid
 *     var(--grey4)` -- a focus ring painted in its own background colour. The MOUSE
 *     hover state one rule below it gets a visible border, so the row a keyboard
 *     operator has landed on is the one state you cannot see. Same ring as everything
 *     else here: 10.65:1 on the row. */
idian--Row.keyFocus {
    border-color: var(--focus_ring);
}

/* 12. SELECTED TEXT INSIDE AN INPUT.
 *     Yami sets `selection-color: var(--text)` on QWidget, which lands mint on the
 *     green selection fill at 2.56:1 -- the same pair rule 1 fixes for item views, in
 *     the one place a stylesheet reaches it through the Qt palette instead. */
QWidget {
    selection-background-color: var(--primary);
    selection-color: var(--ink_on_fill);
}

/* 13. THE T-BAR is the widest single control in the window during a manual
 *     transition, and Yami gives it a height and no colour of its own -- it inherits
 *     the generic slider, groove included. Named here so it is a decision rather than
 *     a leftover: unlit cell for the track, label mint for the bar you drag. */
.slider-tbar::groove:horizontal {
    background-color: var(--grey4);
}

.slider-tbar::handle:horizontal {
    background-color: var(--green1);
}

/* 14. THE OPERATOR PANEL — docks/QCiRigPanes.cpp.
 *
 *     The operator's verdict on the fork before the theme existed was "completely unthemed and
 *     looks exactly like stock fucking OBS." That is the same failure this block prevents for the
 *     panel: a first-party surface built out of bare QPushButtons and QLabels inherits the generic
 *     rules above and reads as a settings dialog, not as the rig's console.
 *
 *     ⚠️ EVERY COLOUR HERE IS A var(). Nothing in this block picks a hex, and the panel's C++
 *     picks none either — it sets a `qciTone` property carrying the rig's OWN word ("bad", "warn",
 *     "go", "live") and this file decides what that word is worth. That is what keeps the panel
 *     inside the provenance gate the rest of the file is held to; the panes could not name a
 *     colour without escaping it.
 *
 *     ⚠️ THE TWO STATE HUES ARE NEVER A SURFACE. The header states it for the whole file — amber
 *     is WARN, red is FAULT, "and neither of them is ever a surface either." So PRIVACY HOLD, the
 *     single most important control in the panel, is a red OUTLINE with red type and fills red
 *     only while it is held down. That is exactly control.html's own `.btn--bad`
 *     (`border-color:var(--c-bad); color:var(--c-bad)` → `:active{background:var(--c-bad)}`), so
 *     the panic control looks the same on the 7" panel and in the dock.
 *
 *     PINK APPEARS EXACTLY ONCE, AND IT IS MONEY: the amount on a moderation card. It used to be
 *     spent on the LIVE state as well, which is the conflict qci-rig/lib/palette.mjs carried as
 *     RIG_VS_PALETTE -- qci-palette.json says "MONEY ONLY, everywhere in the rig", control.html
 *     said "money, LIVE dot, a meter's leading edge", and neither file knew about the other. It is
 *     settled the palette's way: LIVE is --live (amber), the meter tip is the label mint, and pink
 *     buys nothing but money. */

/*     ⚠️ `OBSDock > QCiRigPane` AND NOT JUST `QCiRigPane`, AND THE REASON IS THE CASCADE.
 *     Rule 6 above paints `OBSDock > QWidget` with --s-well, and a pane handed to
 *     QDockWidget::setWidget() IS a direct QWidget child of the dock. Two type selectors beat one
 *     in CSS specificity no matter which is written later, so a bare `QCiRigPane` rule parses,
 *     resolves, matches — and loses, silently, leaving the panel a different shade from the one
 *     this file claims. The bare selector is kept beside it so a pane hosted anywhere else still
 *     gets its ground. */
QCiRigPane,
OBSDock > QCiRigPane,
#qciRigPaneHeader,
#qciRigPaneBody,
#qciRigPaneScroll {
    background-color: var(--bg_window);
}

#qciRigPaneScroll {
    border: none;
}

#qciRigPaneTitle {
    color: var(--green1);
}

/* The pane's hairline. control.html frames a pane with corner brackets, which QSS cannot draw
 * (see the header); a rule between the title and the readout is the reachable echo. */
#qciRigPaneRule {
    border: none;
    max-height: 1px;
    background-color: var(--grey2);
}

#qciRigPaneStatus,
#qciRigChipKey,
#qciRigCardMeta,
#qciRigEmpty,
#qciRigNote,
#qciRigPhase,
#qciRigSay,
#qciRigPrivacy {
    color: var(--text_muted);
}

/* ── PRIVACY HOLD ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
#qciRigPanic {
    color: var(--danger);
    border: 2px solid var(--danger);
    background-color: transparent;
}

#qciRigPanic:hover {
    background-color: var(--red6);
}

#qciRigPanic:pressed {
    background-color: var(--danger);
    color: var(--ink_on_fill);
}

/* ── the tones, wherever they land ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
#qciRigPaneStatus[qciTone="bad"],
#qciRigPrivacy[qciTone="bad"],
#qciRigScene[qciTone="bad"],
#qciRigChipValue[qciTone="bad"],
#qciRigSay[qciTone="bad"] {
    color: var(--danger);
}

#qciRigPaneStatus[qciTone="warn"],
#qciRigPrivacy[qciTone="warn"],
#qciRigChipValue[qciTone="warn"],
#qciRigFace[qciTone="warn"] {
    color: var(--warning);
}

#qciRigPrivacy[qciTone="go"] {
    color: var(--green2);
}

/* ── segmented and picker buttons: outline that FILLS when it is the current one ───────────── */
#qciRigMaskVision:checked,
#qciRigMaskBlock:checked,
#qciRigBrb:checked,
#qciRigMirror:checked,
#qciRigSceneButton:checked {
    background-color: var(--green2);
    color: var(--ink_on_fill);
    border-color: var(--green2);
}

/* ── the panic's third step, and the alarm that says it came undone ──────────────────────────
 *
 * The virtual camera is PINNED in this collection, so it renders its pinned scene regardless of
 * program and only StopVirtualCam takes it off air. The rig answers a hold with one of three
 * words and they are three rig states, not two: "stopped" (it was cut), "idle" (there was
 * nothing to cut -- NOT an alarm, because every alarm that cries on a normal day is one the
 * operator learns to ignore on the day it matters) and "unreachable" (nobody knows). Three
 * words, three tones, and the tone is set in C++ while every colour stays here. */
#qciRigVcam {
    color: var(--text_muted);
    font-size: var(--font_label);
}

#qciRigVcam[qciTone="go"] {
    color: var(--green1);
}

#qciRigVcam[qciTone="bad"] {
    color: var(--danger);
}

/*     THE HOLD-DID-NOT-STICK ALARM. A FILL rather than an outline, and it is the only red fill
 *     at rest anywhere in this application -- everything else danger-coloured is an outline
 *     until it acts. The exception is deliberate: this label appears only when the panic button
 *     the operator just pressed has been undone by the watchdog, which is the single worst
 *     thing this window can have to say. */
#qciRigHoldAlarm[qciTone="bad"] {
    background-color: var(--danger);
    color: var(--ink_on_fill);
    padding: var(--sp_8);
    font-size: var(--font_label);
}

/*     MIRROR'S THIRD STATE NEEDS A SENTENCE, NOT A GREY RECTANGLE. `mirror` is null when the
 *     filter is not provisioned on every camera, when the angles disagree, or when the link is
 *     down -- three reasons for one disabled control, and an operator fighting a switch
 *     mid-stream needs to know which. */
#qciRigMirror:disabled {
    color: var(--text_muted);
    border-color: var(--grey4);
}

/* ── the stream control ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
#qciRigStream[qciTone="go"],
#qciRigApproveAll,
#qciRigReactionAdd {
    background-color: var(--green2);
    color: var(--ink_on_fill);
    border-color: var(--green2);
}

/* ON AIR IS AMBER AND MONEY IS PINK, AND THEY USED TO BE THE SAME COLOUR HERE.
 * The header's old claim -- "PINK APPEARS TWICE AND BOTH ARE ITS DECLARED JOB: money on a
 * moderation card, and the LIVE state" -- was the conflict, not a resolution: the palette
 * restricts pink to money in as many words, and control.html's LIVE dot has moved to amber
 * for the same reason. The card amount below is the only pink left, and it is money. */
#qciRigStream[qciTone="live"],
#qciRigOnAir {
    color: var(--live);
}

#qciRigStream[qciTone="live"] {
    border-color: var(--live);
}

/*     WHERE PINK IS LEGAL, EXHAUSTIVELY. Four spends, and every one of them is a rendered
 *     quantity of money: the donation figure on a moderation card, the goal's current/target
 *     readout, the goal meter's FILLED cells, and the flight strip's goal chip. Pink's entire
 *     value is scarcity -- put it on a meter tip or a LIVE indicator and it is on screen
 *     continuously and therefore signals nothing, which is exactly the failure the token was
 *     written to prevent and which had already happened once. Nothing else in this application
 *     may contain this colour. */
#qciRigCardAmount,
#qciRigGoalFigure {
    color: var(--pink2);
}

/*     THE GOAL METER. A custom-painted widget, so its cells cannot be reached by an ordinary
 *     background rule -- they are Q_PROPERTYs and this is the only place they are set, which is
 *     what keeps the rule "no QCi-authored C++ names a colour" true through a QPainter.
 *     Same cell grammar as the audio meter next door; the FILLED colour is the one difference,
 *     and it is pink because a goal meter is a rendering of money. */
QCiGoalMeter {
    qproperty-cellUnlit: var(--grey4);
    qproperty-cellFilled: var(--pink2);
    qproperty-cellOver: var(--green2);
}

#qciRigStream[qciTone="bad"],
#qciRigReject,
#qciRigRowButtonBad {
    color: var(--danger);
    border-color: var(--danger);
}

#qciRigReject:pressed,
#qciRigRowButtonBad:pressed {
    background-color: var(--danger);
    color: var(--ink_on_fill);
}

#qciRigAuto[qciTone="warn"],
#qciRigMute {
    color: var(--warning);
    border-color: var(--warning);
}

#qciRigApprove,
#qciRigUnmute {
    color: var(--green2);
    border-color: var(--green2);
}

/* ── numbers are cyan, and only numbers ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
#qciRigChipValue {
    color: var(--focus_ring);
}

/* ── list rows ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
/*     THE CARD'S LEFT BRACKET IS ONE STEP BRIGHTER THAN THE CONSOLE'S, AND THAT IS MEASURED.
 *     control.html paints it `border-left: var(--line) solid var(--s-edge)` on `--s-block`, and
 *     --s-edge is rgba(34,197,94,.40) — which composites to about #135C30 and measures ~2.0:1
 *     against the card plate. The console does not audit that pair; this fork does
 *     (qci-rig/lib/studiosurfaces.mjs `card.edge`), and the audit failed the first time this
 *     block was written. --grey2 is the theme's declared HAIRLINE colour, the same value the dock
 *     titlebar rule already uses, and it clears the floor while staying the structure hue. */
#qciRigCard,
#qciRigRow {
    background-color: var(--grey5);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--grey2);
}

#qciRigCard[qciTone="bad"],
#qciRigRow[qciTone="bad"] {
    border-left-color: var(--danger);
}

#qciRigCard[qciTone="warn"],
#qciRigRow[qciTone="warn"] {
    border-left-color: var(--warning);
}

#qciRigCardWho,
#qciRigScene,
#qciRigFace {
    color: var(--green1);
}

#qciRigCardText {
    color: var(--text);
}

#qciRigSubhead {
    color: var(--green2);
}

/* ==================================================================== *
 * 15. TYPOGRAPHY. THE HALF OF THE VERDICT A PALETTE CANNOT ANSWER.
 *
 *     Before this rule the file contained no font declaration of any kind: `grep font QCi.obt`
 *     returned nothing, and every size, face and weight in the application was inherited Yami.
 *     A theme that changes only what the window is COLOURED leaves the window READING exactly
 *     as it did, which is why "completely unthemed" was a fair verdict on a file with 200
 *     sourced colours in it.
 *
 *     THE FAMILY LIST IS WRITTEN LITERALLY AND NOT AS A var(), and that is a parser fact.
 *     ParseThemeVariables() consumes ONE token as a value, so a variable holding
 *     `'Open Sans', '.AppleSystemUIFont'` resolves to `Open Sans` and drops the fallback --
 *     invisible on this Mac, total on one without the face. The QSS body is only
 *     var()-substituted textually, never lexed, so the comma survives here.
 * ==================================================================== */

/*     The application's default. Same family list Yami asks for, restated rather than inherited
 *     so that the base size beside it is a decision and not an accident. */
* {
    font-family: 'Open Sans', '.AppleSystemUIFont', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
    font-size: var(--font_body);
}

/*     LABELS ARE SMALLER THAN BODY, WHICH IS THE INVERSE OF A DOCUMENT AND THE NORM FOR AN
 *     INSTRUMENT. A pane title, a dock title and a chip legend are not read -- they are
 *     recognised -- so they drop to --font_label and let the value beside them be the loud
 *     thing. The CASE and the TRACKING that finish this job are unreachable from QSS and are
 *     applied by ApplyLabelFont() in docks/QCiRigPanes.cpp; this file sets what it can. */
QDockWidget::title,
QGroupBox::title,
QHeaderView::section,
QMenuBar,
#qciRigPaneTitle,
#qciRigSubhead,
#qciRigChipKey,
#qciRigKey {
    font-size: var(--font_label);
}

#qciRigCardMeta,
#qciRigNote,
#qciRigEmpty,
#qciRigPrivacy {
    font-size: var(--font_micro);
}

/*     EVERY NUMBER IN THE APPLICATION IS MENLO, and Menlo specifically: it is at
 *     /System/Library/Fonts/Menlo.ttc and therefore OS-guaranteed, where SF Mono is
 *     dot-prefixed and unreliable to reach from Qt.
 *
 *     A monospaced readout is not decoration here. Telemetry, a dB figure, a timecode and a
 *     countdown all CHANGE while being read, and a proportional face makes the whole field
 *     reflow on every tick -- the digits jitter horizontally and the eye has to re-find them.
 *     Tabular digits hold their columns, so a changing number changes only its glyphs.
 *
 *     The spinboxes are included because a spinbox in this application is always a quantity;
 *     QLineEdit is NOT, because it holds names and URLs, and a URL in a monospace column is
 *     harder to read rather than easier. */
QSpinBox,
QDoubleSpinBox,
#qciRigChipValue,
#qciRigPhase,
#qciRigCardAmount,
#qciRigRxRate {
    font-family: 'Menlo';
    font-size: var(--font_readout);
}

/*     The two controls that are read from across the room rather than at the keyboard. */
#qciRigStream,
#qciRigOnAir,
#qciRigGo {
    font-size: var(--font_go);
}

#qciRigPanic {
    font-size: var(--font_hero);
}

/* ==================================================================== *
 * 16. THE CORNER BRACKET. THE ONE THING THIS FILE SAID IT COULD NOT DRAW.
 *
 *     The rig frames a pane with a TOP-LEFT and a BOTTOM-RIGHT bracket and never four sides,
 *     and the header of this file used to list that as unreachable from QSS. It is reachable.
 *     border-image performs a 9-slice, and on a 24x24 asset sliced 12/12/12/12 the four edge
 *     regions and the centre are all ZERO pixels wide -- so the only thing Qt can paint is the
 *     four corner tiles. themes/QCi/bracket.svg draws two of them and leaves the other two
 *     deliberately empty, and the result is the rig's two-corner frame rather than a box.
 *
 *     ⚠️ THREE WIDGETS. NOT FOUR. A signature that appears on everything is a border. The
 *     three are the operator's privacy block, the scene rail and the program frame -- the
 *     panic, the switcher and the picture. The first two do not exist yet (see rule 18); the
 *     rule is written now so that the widget arrives already wearing it rather than being
 *     retro-fitted by whoever notices.
 *
 *     Requires the qsvg image plugin, which every OBS icon already loads. */
#qciOpPrivacy,
#qciSceneRailBody,
#qciProgramFrame {
    border-image: url(theme:QCi/bracket.svg) 12 12 12 12 stretch stretch;
    border-width: 12px;
    background-color: var(--bg_base);
}

/* ==================================================================== *
 * 17. NOTHING IS ROUNDED, AND UNTIL NOW THAT WAS NOT TRUE.
 *
 *     MEASURED, and it contradicts this file's own claim. The variable block above sets every
 *     radius token to 0 and comments that the rig rounds nothing -- but Yami does not route
 *     all of its radii through those tokens. It writes LITERALS in six places, and every one
 *     of them survived into the shipped theme:
 *
 *       Yami.obt:1107-1113  QTabBar::tab:top / :bottom      4px
 *       Yami.obt:619        QProgressBar                     4px
 *       Yami.obt:1228-1229  QDateTimeEdit::drop-down          4px
 *       Yami.obt:1539-1577  QSlider groove / sub-page / add-page, H and V, incl. disabled  2px
 *
 *     So every tab in the application, every progress bar and every fader track has been
 *     rounded this whole time in a theme whose stated rule is that nothing is. A tab bar is
 *     also the single most recognisably-OBS piece of chrome in the window, so this is not a
 *     pedantic correction: rounded tabs are a large part of why the answer to "did the theme
 *     land" kept being no.
 * ==================================================================== */
QTabBar::tab:top {
    border-top-left-radius: 0px;
    border-top-right-radius: 0px;
}

QTabBar::tab:bottom {
    border-bottom-left-radius: 0px;
    border-bottom-right-radius: 0px;
}

QProgressBar,
QProgressBar::chunk {
    border-radius: 0px;
}

QComboBox::drop-down,
QComboBox::drop-down:editable,
QDateTimeEdit::drop-down,
QDateTimeEdit::drop-down:editable {
    border-top-right-radius: 0px;
    border-bottom-right-radius: 0px;
}

QSlider::groove,
QSlider::groove:horizontal,
QSlider::groove:vertical,
QSlider::sub-page:horizontal,
QSlider::sub-page:horizontal:disabled,
QSlider::sub-page:vertical,
QSlider::sub-page:vertical:disabled,
QSlider::add-page:horizontal,
QSlider::add-page:horizontal:disabled,
QSlider::add-page:vertical,
QSlider::add-page:vertical:disabled {
    border-radius: 0px;
}

/*     THE TAB IS A SEGMENT, NOT A FOLDER TAB. Upstream draws a rounded plate that lifts out of
 *     the bar; the rig draws a flat cell that LIGHTS when it is the current one. Selected takes
 *     a 2px structure rule along its top edge -- the same "lit pane" grammar the dock titlebar
 *     already uses on its left edge -- and sits on the raised block, so the current tab reads by
 *     its rule and its surface rather than by its shape. */
QTabBar::tab {
    border-radius: 0px;
    border: 1px solid var(--tab_border);
    border-top: 2px solid transparent;
    margin-right: var(--sp_2);
}

QTabBar::tab:selected {
    background: var(--grey5);
    color: var(--text);
    border: 1px solid var(--grey3);
    border-top: 2px solid var(--green2);
}

QTabBar::tab:hover {
    border-top: 2px solid var(--grey2);
}

/* ==================================================================== *
 * 18. THE OBJECT NAMES THE LAYOUT OVERHAUL WILL BRING.
 *
 *     ⚠️ HONESTY, BECAUSE A STYLESHEET CANNOT ANNOUNCE ITS OWN DEAD RULES: as of this commit
 *     NONE of the object names in this section exists in the C++. They match nothing, they cost
 *     nothing, and Qt ignores an unmatched selector silently. They are here because the layout
 *     work and the theme work are separate commits, and the alternative -- a new widget landing
 *     unstyled and picking its own hexes in C++ to compensate -- is exactly how a themed
 *     application grows a second, unsourced palette. Every colour below is a var(), so the
 *     widget arrives inside the provenance gate rather than outside it.
 *
 *     Anything in this section that is still unmatched once the layout lands is dead and should
 *     be deleted then.
 * ==================================================================== */

/*     THE FLIGHT STRIP -- a full-width band above both dock columns, which exists in no OBS
 *     build. It is chrome, so it sits on the GROUND, and its bottom border is the window's
 *     off-air/on-air waterline: a hairline normally, and a 2px amber line the full width of
 *     the window while live. */
#qciFlightStrip {
    background: var(--bg_window);
    border: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--grey2);
    spacing: var(--sp_16);
    padding: var(--sp_4) var(--sp_12);
}

#qciFlightStrip[qciTone="live"] {
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--live);
}

/*     ON AIR IS THE LARGEST SINGLE-COLOUR OBJECT IN THE CHROME, and it inverts rather than
 *     recolouring: off air it is a plate with a hairline, on air it is a solid amber field
 *     carrying the one ink. It does not blink -- a field this size strobing at any rate is the
 *     actual photosensitivity hazard, and the small lamp inside it carries the motion. */
#qciFsAir {
    background: var(--grey6);
    color: var(--text);
    border: 1px solid var(--grey2);
    padding: var(--sp_4) var(--sp_8);
}

#qciFsAir[qciTone="live"] {
    background: var(--live);
    color: var(--ink_on_fill);
    border: none;
}

#qciFsClock {
    font-family: 'Menlo';
    font-size: var(--font_clock);
    color: var(--text);
}

#qciFsClock[qciTone="live"] {
    color: var(--live);
}

#qciFsScene {
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: var(--font_label);
}

/*     THE TELEMETRY CHIPS. One tone protocol for the whole application: the C++ sets the rig's
 *     own word and this file decides what the word is worth, so no widget picks a hex. A WARN
 *     chip OUTLINES and a FAULT chip FILLS -- different form, not just different hue, because
 *     the two are read peripherally. */
#qciFsVitals QLabel,
#qciFsMask,
#qciFsWatchdog,
#qciFsQ,
#qciFsA,
#qciFsVod,
#qciFsLamps QLabel {
    background: var(--grey6);
    color: var(--text);
    border: 1px solid var(--grey2);
    padding: var(--sp_4) var(--sp_8);
    font-size: var(--font_micro);
}

#qciFsMask[qciTone="go"],
#qciFsWatchdog[qciTone="go"] {
    color: var(--green2);
    border-color: var(--green2);
}

#qciFsMask[qciTone="warn"],
#qciFsWatchdog[qciTone="warn"] {
    color: var(--warning);
    border-color: var(--warning);
}

#qciFsMask[qciTone="bad"],
#qciFsWatchdog[qciTone="bad"],
#qciFsVod {
    background: var(--danger);
    color: var(--ink_on_fill);
    border: none;
}

/*     Numbers are cyan and money is pink, and those are the only two rules about a figure. */
#qciFsVitals QLabel[qciTone="value"] {
    color: var(--focus_ring);
    font-family: 'Menlo';
    font-size: var(--font_readout);
}

#qciFsGoal {
    color: var(--pink2);
    font-family: 'Menlo';
    font-size: var(--font_readout);
    background: transparent;
    border: none;
}

/*     THE COMMAND BAR. The rig's command line is the best thing on the browser panel and OBS
 *     has nothing like it. It replaces the layout inside the status bar Qt already owns. */
#qciCommandBar {
    background: var(--bg_window);
    border: none;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--grey2);
}

#qciCmdPrompt {
    color: var(--focus_ring);
    font-family: 'Menlo';
    font-size: var(--font_readout);
}

#qciCmdLine {
    background: transparent;
    border: none;
    color: var(--text);
    font-family: 'Menlo';
    font-size: var(--font_readout);
}

#qciCmdOut {
    color: var(--text_muted);
    font-size: var(--font_label);
}

#qciCmdOut[qciTone="bad"] {
    color: var(--danger);
}

#qciCmdOut[qciTone="warn"] {
    color: var(--warning);
}

#qciCmdOut[qciTone="go"] {
    color: var(--green2);
}

/*     THE SCENE RAIL. A switcher, not a file browser: full-width 56px cells, and the one on
 *     program wears the lit bracket. There is no add/remove/duplicate toolbar in this dock --
 *     that is the editor, and the editor is behind a mode. */
#qciRailScene {
    background: var(--grey6);
    color: var(--text);
    border: 1px solid var(--grey2);
    border-left: 6px solid var(--grey6);
    padding: var(--sp_8) var(--sp_12);
    text-align: left;
}

#qciRailScene:hover {
    background: var(--grey5);
    border-color: var(--green2);
    border-left-color: var(--grey5);
}

#qciRailScene:pressed {
    background: var(--green2);
    color: var(--ink_on_fill);
}

#qciRailScene[qciTone="live"] {
    background: var(--grey5);
    color: var(--text);
    border-left: 6px solid var(--green2);
}

/*     THE PROGRAM MONITOR. The letterbox is --qc_backing and not a grey, because that is the
 *     one surface in this application a rendered QC is composited onto -- the adjacency rule
 *     the whole file is organised around. It is the only place that value is legal.
 *     The frame goes amber and thickens while live; the bracket comes from rule 16. */
#qciProgramFrame {
    background: var(--qc_backing);
}

#qciProgramFrame[qciTone="live"] {
    border-image: none;
    border: 3px solid var(--live);
}

#qciPgmBug {
    background: var(--grey7);
    color: var(--text);
    border: 1px solid var(--grey2);
    padding: var(--sp_4) var(--sp_8);
    font-size: var(--font_label);
}

/*     THE OPERATOR SECTION -- one dock replacing six, because six QDockWidgets cost six Qt
 *     title bars plus a Qt tab bar, and a Qt tab bar is the most recognisably-OBS object in the
 *     window. The pager below is NOT a QTabBar for that exact reason. */
#qciOperatorDock,
#qciOpHead,
#qciOpPages,
#qciOpTransport {
    background: var(--bg_window);
}

#qciOpHead {
    color: var(--green1);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--green2);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--grey2);
    padding: var(--sp_4) var(--sp_8);
    font-size: var(--font_label);
}

/*     The five-way page selector. Flat checkable buttons in an exclusive group, lit by a 2px
 *     rule along the TOP edge -- the same grammar as the tab bar in rule 17 and the dock
 *     titlebar in rule 2, so "this one is current" is one visual idea in three places. */
#qciOpPages QPushButton {
    background: var(--grey6);
    color: var(--text_muted);
    border: none;
    border-top: 2px solid transparent;
    padding: var(--sp_8) var(--sp_4);
    font-size: var(--font_label);
}

#qciOpPages QPushButton:hover {
    background: var(--grey5);
}

#qciOpPages QPushButton:checked {
    background: var(--grey5);
    color: var(--text);
    border-top: 2px solid var(--green2);
}

/*     THE AUDIO STRIP. A permanent band, because audio is the failure mode a streamer cannot
 *     see. Operating surface only -- mute, level, and where the audio ends up; the per-source
 *     gear menu and Advanced Audio Properties belong to the editor. */
#qciAudioStripDock,
#qciAudioStripHeader {
    background: var(--bg_window);
}

#qciAudioStripHeader {
    color: var(--text_muted);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--grey2);
    font-size: var(--font_label);
}

#qciAudioRow {
    background: var(--grey6);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--grey4);
}

#qciAudioRowName {
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: var(--font_label);
}

#qciAudioRowDb {
    color: var(--focus_ring);
    font-family: 'Menlo';
    font-size: var(--font_readout);
}

/*     THE DISCRETE LED METER. QCiLedMeter is a VolumeMeter subclass and not a themed
 *     VolumeMeter, because VolumeMeter paints a CONTINUOUS bar in C++ and only its colours are
 *     reachable from here -- the cell geometry is not. These are the same four cell values the
 *     continuous meter uses at rule 3, so the two cannot disagree. */
QCiLedMeter {
    qproperty-cellUnlitColor: var(--green6);
    qproperty-cellLitColor: var(--green2);
    qproperty-cellHotColor: var(--green1);
    qproperty-cellPeakColor: var(--danger);
    qproperty-cellGoalColor: var(--pink2);
}

/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 *     THE STRUCTURAL PASS — because a recolour is not a rework.
 *
 *     Measured before writing this: QCi.obt carried 142 colour declarations against 39 metric
 *     declarations. That ratio IS the operator's complaint ("it just looks like recolored OBS"),
 *     stated as a number. Everything below changes SIZE, WEIGHT and SPACE rather than hue.
 *
 *     Scope note: QSS cannot move a widget, merge two lists, or delete a menu. Those need C++ and
 *     are tracked separately. What QSS CAN do is decide what is loud and what is quiet, and stock
 *     OBS gets that exactly wrong for this rig — every control is the same size, so nothing reads
 *     as more important than anything else.
 * ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

/*     GO LIVE IS NOT A BUTTON IN A LIST OF BUTTONS.
 *
 *     Stock OBS stacks Start Streaming / Start Recording / Start Virtual Camera / Studio Mode /
 *     Settings as five identical grey rectangles. The one that puts the operator's face on the
 *     internet is indistinguishable from the one that opens preferences. This makes the transport
 *     read in a glance from across the desk, which is the distance the operator actually sits at. */
/*     ⚠️ SIZED TO SURVIVE A NARROW DOCK, NOT SIZED TO LOOK GOOD IN A WIDE ONE.
 *
 *     The first version of this rule used font-size 17px with letter-spacing 1.5px. It read
 *     beautifully until the operator dock landed beside it and squeezed the Controls dock, at which
 *     point the label rendered as "TART STREAMIN" — QPushButton does not wrap or elide its text, it
 *     just clips both ends, so the single most important control in the window became a fragment of
 *     a word. A transport button that cannot survive its container being resized is not a transport
 *     button, and dock widths are the operator's to change at any time.
 *
 *     So: weight and height carry the emphasis (they cost no horizontal space), while font-size and
 *     letter-spacing stay modest. It is still unmistakably the loudest control in the dock — the
 *     secondary buttons below are 30px at label size — without betting on a width nobody promised. */
#streamButton {
    min-height: 44px;
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0px;
    padding-left: 4px;
    padding-right: 4px;
    border: 2px solid var(--green2);
    border-radius: 0px;
}

#streamButton:hover {
    border-color: var(--green1);
}

/*     ...and the rest of the transport is SECONDARY. Not hidden — recording and the virtual camera
 *     are real controls — but visibly a tier below the thing that starts the broadcast. */
#recordButton,
#virtualCamButton,
#replayBufferButton,
#broadcastButton {
    min-height: 30px;
    font-size: var(--font_label);
}

/*     Settings and Studio Mode are NOT transport at all. They are the two controls most likely to
 *     be hit by accident while reaching for Go Live, so they get the quietest treatment in the
 *     dock and stop competing for the eye. */
#settingsButton,
#modeSwitch {
    min-height: 26px;
    font-size: var(--font_label);
    color: var(--text_muted);
    border: 1px solid var(--grey3);
}

/*     THE SCENE RAIL HAS TO BE READABLE WHILE STANDING UP.
 *
 *     This collection is 24 scenes deep and stock row metrics render them as a dense undifferentiated
 *     column — PRIVACY HOLD looks exactly like QCi Boot. Taller rows, a real selected state with a
 *     structure-green left rule (the same device QDockWidget::title uses above, so selection reads as
 *     "this region" rather than as a highlight), and enough leading to scan without reading. */
SceneTree::item,
SourceTree::item {
    min-height: 26px;
    padding-left: 6px;
    border-left: 3px solid transparent;
}

SceneTree::item:selected,
SourceTree::item:selected {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--green1);
    font-weight: 600;
}

SceneTree::item:hover,
SourceTree::item:hover {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--green3);
}

/*     RECLAIM THE DOCK CHROME. Each QDockWidget spends its title bar before showing a single row,
 *     and the fork now TABIFIES the builtin docks (QCiBasic_Docks.cpp), so a tab bar and a title bar
 *     stack on the same pane. padding_large on a title that is already redundant with its tab is the
 *     most expensive pixel in the window; this halves it without removing the title. */
QDockWidget::title {
    padding: 3px 6px;
}

/*     The tab bar those docks now share should read as structure, not as decoration. */
QTabBar::tab {
    min-height: 22px;
    padding: 4px 12px;
    font-size: var(--font_label);
    letter-spacing: 0.5px;
}

QTabBar::tab:selected {
    font-weight: 700;
}
